 \~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 \Download code for OSBYTE *TAPE trap. Copied to notape by *QUPCFS
 
-.osb_s
-	CMP	#&8C		\*TAPE ?
-	BNE	osb_j		\no, continue to OSBYTE
-	RTS	    		\yes, return no action
-
-.osb_j	JMP	&0000		\continue to OSBYTE at OS vector..
-		        		\..address written by *QUPCFS
-.osb_e	NOP
+\The trap is in two pieces because &03D1 belongs to the MOS and is rewritten
+\behind our back. The entry sits in the eight bytes the chunk header vacated and
+\jumps to the body, which starts past the MOS byte. Both are absolute jumps, so
+\each piece is position independent once copied.
+.osb_s
+	CMP	#&8C		\*TAPE ?
+	BEQ	osb_eat		\yes, swallow it and keep WiCFS selected
+	JMP	notape_body
+.osb_eat
+	RTS
+.osb_e
+
+\Body, copied to notape_body. A carries the caller's OSBYTE reason code, so it
+\is saved before the signature is compared. The comparison is a full eight bits:
+\a BIT test of the top two bits matches A9, AD, A5, A2 and A0, which are among
+\the commonest opcodes, and would call game code.
+.osb_b_s
+	PHA			\A carries the caller's OSBYTE reason code
+	LDA	romsel+(guard_sig-s_guard)
+	NOP			\aligns the next operand onto the MOS byte at &03D1
+.osb_hole
+	BIT	&00		\operand is &03D1, which the MOS rewrites. A zero-page read
+				\has no side effect and BIT leaves A alone; the flags it
+				\sets are replaced by the compare below.
+	CMP	#&A5		\is the repair helper still ours?
+	BNE	osb_restore	\no, a loader replaced it; do not call it
+	JSR	romsel+(guard_entry-s_guard)
+.osb_restore
+	PLA
+.osb_j	JMP	&0000		\continue to OSBYTE at OS vector..
+		        		\..address written by *QUPCFS
+.osb_b_e
+
 
